+A small dsh **bundle-plugin** sample: the Node half lives under `.dsh-plugin/`,
+the root manifest `package.json` declares `dsh.bundle.patch → ./cordis.patch.yml`
+whose `insert` row mounts the Node half as an ordinary Cordis plugin, and the
+browser half is a self-executing script that the Node half serves at
+`/pet/ui.js` and injects into the page through the `webServer.tapIndex` index
+hook. In the page the script appends the `#bench-pet` element. The route answers
+`GET`/`HEAD` with the script (`application/javascript`, `cache-control:
+no-store`) and every other method with `405`.
+
+Install the checkout into a browser-facing profile and inspect the applied
+layer (`web` is the shipped profile template composed from
+`@deepseek-ai/dsh-base` and `@deepseek-ai/dsh-web-app`):
+
+```sh
+dsh plugin --profile web add /path/to/this/checkout
+dsh --profile web --dump-config   # shows the "@demo/dsh-bench-repo" layer
+```
+
+`webServer` is an optional contribution (a scoped `ctx.inject` inside `apply`),
+so the plugin activates in every profile: in a headless composition (for example
+`@deepseek-ai/dsh-base` plus `@deepseek-ai/dsh-headless`) it registers nothing,
+and in a browser-facing profile it adds the route and the index tag as soon as
+the `webServer` service is available.
+
+`.dsh-plugin/package.json` no longer declares a `dsh` key: on this release the
+only manifest dsh reads is the root `package.json`.
 
 The host in this container is dsh **0.1.2-alpha.2**.
 
